From 57227621d20ca3d6a1fe36945ddbbc2094e20f3f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ebg-ant <296364998+ebg-ant@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 05:20:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat: Arbor ships knowing how small the stock apps go, as a
 hosted Mac measured them
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The store cures the second day; from an empty home every app is still a stranger. Now what a hosted Mac measured of 32 apps that come
with macOS 26 ships with Arbor, so that a first minute meets no stranger among them.

- collab/evidence/app-sizes/macos-26.tsv is the run's file as it came back (scripts/mac-shell/app-sizes.sh); scripts/app-sizes.py makes
  AppSizes+Shipped.swift of the lines whose verdict is `clean`, less those during which Accessibility reported an error and windows of one
  size (35 clean, 32 ship). Lint fails if the two disagree.
- It holds for the major version of macOS it was measured on: measured, not guessed. It is foresight like the rest, asked about in the
  same places and gone by exactly as if it had been learned. What was learned on this Mac outranks it, also where that says that
  nothing is known: what a window disproves of the table is kept, or the next window would be foreseen by the table again.
- `relayout` forgets what is kept, and of an app that is open and in the table it leaves the word that nothing is known.
- Nothing is written because something ships. A trace tells what shipped like what was learned, a day old, and a replay goes by its
  trace whatever the Arbor that replays it ships with. Tests know nothing unless they say so.
- arbor-sim: `ships <macOS>|nothing`, and `open <App> … as <bundle id>`: a window that goes as small as the Mac measured.

Board 1.32, the second step. Spec: "how small apps go" under docs/features.
